2026-06-30 15:43:34,454 - openai._base_client - DEBUG - Request options: {'method': 'post', 'url': '/chat/completions', 'timeout': 300, 'files': None, 'idempotency_key': 'stainless-python-retry-f0e07bba-a0c7-4d09-98d4-e171deaeb898', 'security': {'bearer_auth': True}, 'content': None, 'json_data': {'messages': [{'role': 'system', 'content': 'You are an expert proteomics assistant. Return valid JSON only.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'You are a proteomics expert analyzing crosslinking mass spectrometry (XL-MS) datasets.\n\nYour task is to cluster raw spectral files into biologically meaningful groups.\n\nA cluster should represent a unique combination of:\n1. Biological / experimental condition\n   - Examples: treatment, control, carbon source, growth medium, cell state, tissue, disease state, time point, perturbation, purified complex, recombinant standard set.\n2. Source organism of the proteome being analyzed\n   - Return the full scientific name.\n   - Use the organism of the biological sample or proteome of interest, not necessarily the expression host.\n   - Example: if human proteins are expressed in E. coli and analyzed as human recombinant standards, the source organism is Homo sapiens.\n   - If the dataset intentionally contains multiple source organisms, create separate clusters by organism when possible.\n3. Crosslinking reagent\n   - Examples: BS3, DSS, DSSO, DSBU, formaldehyde.\n   - If multiple crosslinkers are used, create separate clusters by reagent when possible.\n\nDo NOT split clusters by:\n- fraction number\n- LC fraction\n- technical replicate\n- biological replicate ID\n- injection number\n- file numbering\n- enrichment fraction\n- instrument run order\n- search batch\n- calibration/QC files\n\nReplicates, fractions, and repeated injections from the same biological condition should be grouped together.\n\n---\n\nINPUTS\n\nRaw filenames:\nRef7090_MS_EJ_10_20211210.raw\nRef7090_MS_LW_01_20211210.raw\nRef7090_MS_LW_02_20211210.raw\nRef7090_MS_LW_03_20211210.raw\nRef7090_MS_LW_04_20211210.raw\nRef7090_MS_LW_05_20211210.raw\nRef7090_MS_LW_06_20211210.raw\nRef7090_MS_LW_07_20211210.raw\nRef7090_MS_LW_08_20211210.raw\nRef7090_MS_LW_09_20211210.raw\n\nPublication text and PRIDE metadata:\n[TITLE]\nBioID of the proteins CGP and FRM1 from Toxoplasma gondii\n\n[ABSTRACT]\nThe conoid complex, a unique structure in the phylum Apicomplexa, is crucial for host cell invasion, egress, and motility. Despite its importance, the detailed molecular composition and assembly mechanisms of the conoid complex remain poorly understood. In previous research, we identified an essential conoidal protein, CGP, which is required for parasite motility activation through a splitCas9 phenotypic screen. Here, using proximity-dependent biotin identification of CGP and FRM1, we uncovered novel components localizing at the preconoidal rings. Furthermore, we identified a novel daughter-specific protein localizing at the APRs. Depletion of this protein resulted in severe morphological defects and failure in daughter bud formation. Ultrastructure expansion microscopy revealed highly disorganized or absent nascent tubulin. Although conoidal proteins could form, they were defective in assembling the conoid complex. Collectively, our findings identify a novel daughter-specific apical polar ring protein that provides the base for the initiation and growth of nascent tubulin, which in turn provides the scaffold for daughter bud formation.\n\n[METHODS]\nThe HFFs were heavily infected with parasites (cgp-TurboID/frm1-3HA, frm1-TurboID/loxPcgp-Halo or DiCreΔku80). After 24 hours post infection, the parasites were treated for 6 hours with or without 150 μM biotin. They were then mechanically released, filtered through 3-μm filters, and washed three times with cold PBS. A total of 6 x 107 parasites were pelleted to purify biotinylated proteins. Additionally, 107 parasites treated with ±150 μM biotin were collected for biotinylation analysis via Western blot. All harvesting steps were performed on ice or at 0–4 °C. Parasite pellets were kept at -80 °C before being used in subsequent steps. To purify biotinylated proteins, 6 x 107 parasites were lysed for 30 minutes in 950 μL of RIPA buffer with Pierce™ protease inhibitor. The supernatant from the lysis was incubated with 50 μL of beads per sample (Dynabeads™ MyOne™ Streptavidin T1, Invitrogen), prewashed with PBS, for 30 minutes at room temperature while being gently rotated. The beads were then washed five times with 1 mL of RIPA buffer without TX-100 but with protease inhibitor, followed by three washes with 50 mM Tris-HCl (pH 8). Washing steps were performed in a cold room. While 10% of the beads were preserved for Western blot analysis, the rest of the beads were pelleted and stored at -80 °C before being sent for mass spectrometry. Immunoprecipitated proteins were quantified as described before with minor changes (Singer et al. 2022). Briefly, the beads were treated with 10 ng/L of trypsin in 1 M urea and 50 mM NH4HCO3 for 30 minutes, then rinsed with 50 mM NH4HCO3. Afterwards, the supernatant was digested overnight with 1 mM DTT. Before LC-MS analysis, the peptides were alkylated and desalted after digestion.  For LC-MS/MS, the peptides were injected into an Ultimate 3000 RSLCnano system and separated in a 25-cm analytical column (75\u2009µm ID, 1.6\u2009µm C18, Aurora-IonOpticks) with a 50-minute gradient from 2 to 35% acetonitrile in 0.1% formic acid. The effluent from the HPLC was directly electrosprayed directly into a Qexactive HF instrument operating in data-dependent mode to automatically transition between full-scan mass spectrometry (MS) and MS/MS acquisition. Survey full-scan MS spectra (from m/z 375–1600) were acquired with resolution R=60,000 at m/z 400 (AGC target of 3x106). The 10 most intense peptide ions with charge states between 2 and 5 were sequentially isolated to a target value of 1x105 and fragmented at collision energy normalised to 27%.\n\n\nPreviously extracted organism(s) and NCBI taxid(s):\nToxoplasma gondii rh\n\n---\n\nTASKS\n\n1. Identify the distinct biological/experimental conditions described by the publication, PRIDE metadata, and filenames.\n\n2. Assign every raw file to exactly one cluster.\n\n3. Define clusters using the smallest number of biologically meaningful groups. Do not over-cluster based only on replicate, fraction, or run-number differences.\n\n4. For each cluster, report:\n   - cluster_id: "cluster_1", "cluster_2", etc.\n   - cluster_label: short machine-readable label using lowercase words separated by underscores\n   - description: concise human-readable summary of the condition\n   - organism: full scientific name of the proteome/sample organism\n   - taxid: NCBI Taxonomy CURIE, e.g. "NCBITaxon:9606"; use null if unknown\n   - crosslinker: crosslinking reagent; use null if unknown\n   - biological_condition: short phrase describing the treatment/control/state\n   - files: list of raw filenames assigned to this cluster\n   - confidence: "high", "medium", or "low"\n   - evidence_quote: exact sentence or short phrase from the publication, PRIDE metadata, or filename pattern that supports this cluster assignment\n   - reasoning: brief explanation of why these files belong together\n\n5. Assign files that cannot be confidently interpreted to cluster_0 with:\n   - cluster_label: "unassigned"\n   - confidence: "low"\n   - evidence_quote: null\n   - reasoning: explain why assignment was not possible\n\n6. QC, calibrant, blank, wash, or test files should be assigned to cluster_0 unless the publication clearly states they are part of the biological experiment.\n\n7. If all biological files appear to come from one condition, create only cluster_1 plus cluster_0 if there are unassigned/QC files.\n\n8. Confidence guidelines:\n   - "high": filenames and publication/metadata agree clearly\n   - "medium": assignment is likely but partially inferred from filenames or incomplete metadata\n   - "low": weak evidence, ambiguous naming, missing metadata, or conflicting information\n\n9. If publication text and filenames conflict, prioritize the publication/PRIDE metadata, but mention the conflict in reasoning.\n\n10. Return ONLY valid JSON. Do not include markdown, comments, or explanatory text outside the JSON.\n\n---\n\nOUTPUT JSON STRUCTURE\n\n{\n  "clusters": [\n    {\n      "cluster_id": "cluster_1",\n      "cluster_label": "yeast_glucose_bs3",\n      "description": "Saccharomyces cerevisiae samples grown in glucose-rich medium and crosslinked with BS3.",\n      "organism": "Saccharomyces cerevisiae",\n      "taxid": "NCBITaxon:4932",\n      "crosslinker": "BS3",\n      "biological_condition": "glucose-rich medium",\n      "files": [\n        "YeastGlu_R1.raw",\n        "YeastGlu_R2.raw",\n        "YeastGlu_fraction_01.raw"\n      ],\n      "confidence": "high",\n      "evidence_quote": "Yeast cells were cultured in glucose- or glycerol-containing medium and crosslinked with BS3.",\n      "reasoning": "The filenames contain Glu and the publication states that glucose-grown yeast samples were crosslinked with BS3."\n    },\n    {\n      "cluster_id": "cluster_0",\n      "cluster_label": "unassigned",\n      "description": "Files that could not be confidently assigned to a biological XL-MS condition.",\n      "organism": null,\n      "taxid": null,\n      "crosslinker": null,\n      "biological_condition": null,\n      "files": [\n        "calibrant.raw",\n        "QC.raw"\n      ],\n      "confidence": "low",\n      "evidence_quote": null,\n      "reasoning": "These files appear to be QC or calibration runs rather than biological samples."\n    }\n  ],\n  "summary": {\n    "n_input_files": 5,\n    "n_assigned_files": 3,\n    "n_unassigned_files": 2,\n    "n_biological_clusters": 1,\n    "overall_confidence": "high"\n  }\n}'}], 'model': 'gpt-5.4-nano-2026-03-17', 'store': True, 'temperature': 0.3}}
