2026-06-30 15:43:48,617 - openai._base_client - DEBUG - Request options: {'method': 'post', 'url': '/chat/completions', 'timeout': 300, 'files': None, 'idempotency_key': 'stainless-python-retry-d34040a6-da7d-4b35-aef5-c3f3c6ec83ab', 'security': {'bearer_auth': True}, 'content': None, 'json_data': {'messages': [{'role': 'system', 'content': 'You are an expert SDRF-Proteomics annotator for crosslinking mass spectrometry (XL-MS) datasets.\n\nExtract the following SDRF metadata terms related to the mass spectrometry acquisition.\n\nTERMS TO EXTRACT:\n─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\nterm: instrument\nsdrf_column: comment[instrument]\nontology: MS:1000031 (PSI-MS)\ndescription: Mass spectrometer instrument make and model.\ninstructions: Use PSI-MS ontology. If multiple instruments, return a list.\n  Common instruments (name → accession):\n    Orbitrap Astral → MS:1003356\n    Orbitrap Exploris 480 → MS:1003028\n    Orbitrap Eclipse → MS:1003349\n    Orbitrap Fusion Lumos → MS:1002732\n    Orbitrap Fusion → MS:1002416\n    Q Exactive HF-X → MS:1002877\n    Q Exactive HF → MS:1002523\n    Q Exactive Plus → MS:1002634\n    Q Exactive → MS:1001911\n    LTQ Orbitrap Velos → MS:1001742\n    LTQ Orbitrap XL → MS:1000556\n    timsTOF → MS:1003005\n    timsTOF Pro → MS:1003230\n    TripleTOF 6600 → MS:1002583\n  Format: NT=<instrument name>;AC=<MS accession>\n\nterm: proteomics data acquisition method\nsdrf_column: comment[proteomics data acquisition method]\nontology: PRIDE:0000659\ndescription: Mass spectrometry data acquisition method.\ninstructions: For XL-MS this is nearly always DDA. Use:\n  NT=data-dependent acquisition;AC=PRIDE:0000041 — for DDA/shotgun\n  NT=data-independent acquisition;AC=PRIDE:0000449 — for DIA (rare in XL-MS)\n  NT=targeted acquisition;AC=PRIDE:0000450 — for SRM/PRM\n  Default to DDA if not stated.\n\nterm: dissociation method\nsdrf_column: comment[dissociation method]\nontology: MS:1000044 (PSI-MS children)\ndescription: Fragmentation method used for MS/MS.\ninstructions: Use PSI-MS terms. If multiple methods, return a list.\n  Common methods:\n    HCD → NT=HCD;AC=MS:1000422\n    CID → NT=CID;AC=MS:1002472\n    ETD → NT=ETD;AC=MS:1000598\n    EThcD → NT=EThcD;AC=MS:1002678\n    UVPD → NT=UVPD;AC=MS:1003247\n    stepped HCD → NT=HCD;AC=MS:1000422 (stepped HCD is still HCD)\n\nterm: collision energy\nsdrf_column: comment[collision energy]\nontology: MS:1000045\ndescription: Collision energy used for fragmentation.\npattern: {value} {unit} where unit is NCE, %, or eV.\n  Stepped HCD: {value} {unit};{value} {unit};{value} {unit}\n  Examples: "30 NCE", "30% NCE", "27 eV", "25 NCE;27 NCE;30 NCE"\ninstructions: Return null if not stated.\n\nterm: dia method\nsdrf_column: comment[dia method]\nontology: PRIDE:0000450\ndescription: Specific DIA method variant (only relevant for DIA datasets).\ninstructions: Return null for DDA XL-MS datasets (which is nearly all XL-MS).\n  If DIA is used, specify the variant (e.g. "SWATH", "MSe", "diaPASEF").\n\nterm: fragment mass tolerance\nsdrf_column: comment[fragment mass tolerance]\nontology: PRIDE:0000576\ndescription: Fragment ion mass tolerance for the database search.\npattern: number + ppm or Da (e.g. "20 ppm", "0.02 Da")\ninstructions: Return null if not stated. Typical XL-MS values: 10–20 ppm for\n  Orbitrap instruments, 0.02–0.05 Da for ion trap.\n\nterm: precursor mass tolerance\nsdrf_column: comment[precursor mass tolerance]\nontology: PRIDE:0000575\ndescription: Precursor ion mass tolerance for the database search.\npattern: number + ppm or Da (e.g. "10 ppm", "5 ppm")\ninstructions: Return null if not stated. Typical: 5–10 ppm.\n\nterm: technical replicate\nsdrf_column: comment[technical replicate]\nontology: MS:1001808\ndescription: Identifier for the technical replicate (integer).\ninstructions: Describe the technical replication strategy found in the text\n  (e.g. "2 technical replicates per sample", "no technical replicates stated").\n  Actual integers are assigned per-file automatically.\n\nterm: data file\nsdrf_column: comment[data file]\nontology: MS:1003083\ndescription: Name of the raw data file (.raw, .wiff, .d).\ninstructions: This is always auto-derived from the PRIDE file list.\n  Describe the file naming convention you observe (e.g. "sample_condition_rep.raw").\n─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n\nReturn a single JSON object where each key is the exact sdrf_column string shown above.\nFor each key, return a dict with:\n  "value": the extracted value (string, list, or null)\n  "accession": ontology accession if resolvable, else null\n  "confidence": "high", "medium", or "low"\n  "evidence_quote": short verbatim quote from the text, or null\n  "evidence_location": location in the paper (e.g. "Methods"), or null'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': '[TITLE]\nBioID of the proteins CGP and FRM1 from Toxoplasma gondii\n\n[ABSTRACT]\nThe conoid complex, a unique structure in the phylum Apicomplexa, is crucial for host cell invasion, egress, and motility. Despite its importance, the detailed molecular composition and assembly mechanisms of the conoid complex remain poorly understood. In previous research, we identified an essential conoidal protein, CGP, which is required for parasite motility activation through a splitCas9 phenotypic screen. Here, using proximity-dependent biotin identification of CGP and FRM1, we uncovered novel components localizing at the preconoidal rings. Furthermore, we identified a novel daughter-specific protein localizing at the APRs. Depletion of this protein resulted in severe morphological defects and failure in daughter bud formation. Ultrastructure expansion microscopy revealed highly disorganized or absent nascent tubulin. Although conoidal proteins could form, they were defective in assembling the conoid complex. Collectively, our findings identify a novel daughter-specific apical polar ring protein that provides the base for the initiation and growth of nascent tubulin, which in turn provides the scaffold for daughter bud formation.\n\n[METHODS]\nThe HFFs were heavily infected with parasites (cgp-TurboID/frm1-3HA, frm1-TurboID/loxPcgp-Halo or DiCreΔku80). After 24 hours post infection, the parasites were treated for 6 hours with or without 150 μM biotin. They were then mechanically released, filtered through 3-μm filters, and washed three times with cold PBS. A total of 6 x 107 parasites were pelleted to purify biotinylated proteins. Additionally, 107 parasites treated with ±150 μM biotin were collected for biotinylation analysis via Western blot. All harvesting steps were performed on ice or at 0–4 °C. Parasite pellets were kept at -80 °C before being used in subsequent steps. To purify biotinylated proteins, 6 x 107 parasites were lysed for 30 minutes in 950 μL of RIPA buffer with Pierce™ protease inhibitor. The supernatant from the lysis was incubated with 50 μL of beads per sample (Dynabeads™ MyOne™ Streptavidin T1, Invitrogen), prewashed with PBS, for 30 minutes at room temperature while being gently rotated. The beads were then washed five times with 1 mL of RIPA buffer without TX-100 but with protease inhibitor, followed by three washes with 50 mM Tris-HCl (pH 8). Washing steps were performed in a cold room. While 10% of the beads were preserved for Western blot analysis, the rest of the beads were pelleted and stored at -80 °C before being sent for mass spectrometry. Immunoprecipitated proteins were quantified as described before with minor changes (Singer et al. 2022). Briefly, the beads were treated with 10 ng/L of trypsin in 1 M urea and 50 mM NH4HCO3 for 30 minutes, then rinsed with 50 mM NH4HCO3. Afterwards, the supernatant was digested overnight with 1 mM DTT. Before LC-MS analysis, the peptides were alkylated and desalted after digestion.  For LC-MS/MS, the peptides were injected into an Ultimate 3000 RSLCnano system and separated in a 25-cm analytical column (75\u2009µm ID, 1.6\u2009µm C18, Aurora-IonOpticks) with a 50-minute gradient from 2 to 35% acetonitrile in 0.1% formic acid. The effluent from the HPLC was directly electrosprayed directly into a Qexactive HF instrument operating in data-dependent mode to automatically transition between full-scan mass spectrometry (MS) and MS/MS acquisition. Survey full-scan MS spectra (from m/z 375–1600) were acquired with resolution R=60,000 at m/z 400 (AGC target of 3x106). The 10 most intense peptide ions with charge states between 2 and 5 were sequentially isolated to a target value of 1x105 and fragmented at collision energy normalised to 27%.\n'}], 'model': 'gpt-5.4-nano-2026-03-17', 'store': True, 'temperature': 0.3}}
